  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Ashok Kumar vs State on 10 August, 2017 Author: Chief Justice Bench: Chief Justice High Court Of Judicature For Rajasthan At Jodhpur S.B. Criminal Revision No. 435 / 1997 Ashok Kumar Jain s/o Dhan Rajji Jain, R/o Lasdavan Tehsil Nimbahera, District Chittorgarh. ----Petitioner Versus State of Rajasthan ----Respondent _____________________________________________________ For Petitioner(s) : Mr. Manish Shishodia. For Respondent(s) : Mr. J.P. Bhardwaj, Pp. _____________________________________________________ Hon'Ble The Chief Justice Order 10/08/2017 1.",
    "facts": "On 21.10.1984 a sample of sesames oil was taken by the Food Inspector from the shop of the petitioner at the bus stand. The sample was opined to be adulterated on account of olice acid being found to be 4.5% as against 3% prescribed by the standard. The defence of the petitioner, even put to the witnesses of the prosecution was that the petitioner was not selling sesames oil. He was selling cigarettes and betel leaves (Pan) from the shop. As per him the Food Inspector used to purchase cigarettes from him on credit and when he demanded money the Food Inspector took the sample from his house where he had kept the sesames oil for domestic consumption. 2. Convicted vide decision dated 14.03.1995 and sentenced to undergo S.I. for a period of six months, the appeal filed has been dismissed on 20.09.1997.",
    "arguments": "3. Contention of learned counsel for the petitioner is that notwithstanding both Courts noting the defence, have not dealt with the evidence relatable thereto. 4. Learned counsel for the petitioner draws my attention to the testimony of Madan Singh (PW-3) who during cross-examination admitted that all documentation took place at the house of the petitioner. The Food Inspector Nemi Chand examined as PW-2 during cross-examination evaded the issue by saying that he was not aware whether the petitioner's shop and residence were at a different places.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Contention of learned counsel for the petitioner is that notwithstanding both Courts noting the defence, have not dealt with the evidence relatable thereto. 4. Learned counsel for the petitioner draws my attention to the testimony of Madan Singh (PW-3) who during cross-examination admitted that all documentation took place at the house of the petitioner. The Food Inspector Nemi Chand examined as PW-2 during cross-examination evaded the issue by saying that he was not aware whether the petitioner's shop and residence were at a different places.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
